  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nBehind the cold front that went through yesterday, a surface high \npressure will be moving through ND and northern MN today. This will \nhelp to turn the winds to be from the east over central and \nnortheastern SD and warm the temperatures slightly. A small upper-\nlevel ridge will be moving over SD tonight through Wednesday. This, \nalong with southerly flow at the surface, will help to warm \ntemperatures Tuesday and Wednesday to be about 10-15 degrees warmer \nthan normal for this time of the year, with highs in the mid 60s to \nthe low 70s. A weak surface low pressure and frontal boundary will \nmove over SD Tuesday night into Wednesday. This low pressure will \nnot do too much over central and northeastern SD, other than causing \nthe winds to turn. This is because of the lack of moisture in the \natmosphere keeping rain from develop.\n\nBehind this surface low pressure, another low pressure is developing \nto the west. As this second low pressure moves towards SD Thursday \nthrough Saturday, it will cause the pressure gradient over SD to \ntighten. This tight pressure gradient will cause the winds to \nstrengthen Thursday afternoon through Saturday, with the strongest \nwinds occurring Friday. There is a 40-70% chance for wind to gust up \nto or over 45 mph over northeastern SD. These winds are something to \nkeep an eye on, because if the models continue to show these \nstronger winds, a wind advisory might be needed. In the current \nmodel runs, the models keep this low pressure to the northwest of \nSD, though they vary the track slightly. They also have multiple \nfrontal boundaries that are tied to this low that move through \ncentral and northeastern SD Thursday night into Saturday. Rain and \npotentially some storms could form along these fronts as they move \nthrough. However, the models vary quite a bit on which fronts have \nrain, the timing, the track, how widespread, and the intensity. \nBecause of the variability, there is only a 20-40% chance for \nprecipitation Thursday night through Saturday. An eye will need to \nbe kept on the upcoming model runs to see if they come to more of an \nagreement and if chances increase or decrease.",
